@@ -188,7 +188,7 @@ Claude delegation has its own configurable billing route, independent of the sub
 
 **Codex-led sessions: how the backstops hold.** Everything above is Claude Code leading and Codex answering a single question. When Codex itself leads a session, the same two backstops have to hold with a different runtime behind them, and they do — through a root-owned system policy baked into the image at `/etc/codex/`, not through anything in your Mac's `~/.codex`. `requirements.toml` there is a set of constraints, not defaults: it clamps whatever a user or project config asks for, pins the unhooked tool surfaces off (unified exec's `write_stdin`, the JS REPL, sub-agents, apps), forbids every MCP server with an empty allowlist, disables hosted web search — which the firewall cannot see, since it is fetched by OpenAI rather than by this container — and declares `/etc/codex/hooks` the only place hooks may come from, so a project cannot add or disable one. Those managed hooks run `codex-guard-adapter`, which feeds Codex's `PreToolUse` JSON into the **same, unmodified** `guard-bash.sh` and `guard-fs.sh` the Claude Code hooks use (Codex's hook names are already Claude-shaped: `Bash` with `tool_input.command`, `apply_patch` aliased `Write`/`Edit`), and turns every `ask` into a `deny` — an unattended session has nobody to answer a prompt, so the safe answer is no. Stated plainly: **Codex's own sandbox cannot run here.** It is bubblewrap, it needs unprivileged user namespaces, and the container does not have them, so a mode below `danger-full-access` would fail every tool call rather than contain it. What holds instead is the container boundary, the firewall, root ownership the `node` user cannot touch, an argv-prefix exec policy for force-pushes, and those hooks — exactly the set that already holds a Claude Code session under `--permission-mode bypassPermissions`. Because Codex fails *open* when a hook binary cannot be spawned, `codex-guard-liveness` exists to refuse that silence: it checks every file in the chain for root ownership and permissions, then pushes real known-bad calls through the real adapter and requires real denials before a Codex-led session is considered safe to start. `docs/codex-tool-inventory.md` lists every Codex tool at the pinned version against the layer that mediates it, including the two rows that are honestly marked unmediated.
 
-**Skills in a Codex-led container.** `$vs`, `$vss` and `$vsss` are Codex skills baked into the image at `/etc/codex/skills/{vs,vss,vsss}/SKILL.md` — the Admin scope root, so a project cannot add, shadow or disable one, and nothing is ever written into your Mac's `~/.codex` or `~/.agents` to make this work. Each SKILL.md tells Codex to read the single-sourced command body at `/usr/local/share/vibe/commands/<name>.md` and follow it verbatim, substituting a fixed table of Codex equivalents for the handful of Claude-only primitives those bodies use (`Agent`, `Skill`, `ScheduleWakeup`, and so on). The one primitive with no Codex equivalent at all is subagent dispatch — Codex's own `multi_agent` is pinned off in this image (see above) — so `/vs`'s Planner/Spec Critic/Generator/Tester/Evaluator roles become `vibe-delegate role <role> --model <astra|opus|sonnet|haiku|fable> --cwd <workspace>`: one fresh `codex exec` or `claude -p` process per role, run under the same hooks and firewall as the lead, never resuming or sharing a session with it. This ships the plumbing and its offline tests only; the first live Codex-led `/vs` trial is a later, separate item (see **MANUAL-TESTS Test 55**). `/vs --panel N` has no subagents to fan out to either, so the image also ships `/usr/local/bin/codex-panel`: `codex-panel run --n N < diff` starts N independent, tool-less `codex exec` reviewers — one process each, a private temp cwd each, the same diff and a per-reviewer nonce on stdin — and prints one JSON object of verdicts for the chair to adjudicate. Its `--verify` mode drops `--ephemeral` so each reviewer's rollout is kept and can be proved to hold only its own nonce, which costs N live calls and leaves the diff and the nonces in `~/.codex/sessions` until you delete them, so it is an opt-in proof rather than the default. The same `$vs`/`$vss`/`$vsss` spelling also works when Claude Code is the lead: a synced CLAUDE.md fragment (`devcontainer/claude-md/dollar-prefix.md`) tells it that a prompt whose first token is one of those means invoke the matching command with the rest of the prompt as its arguments, verbatim.
+**Skills in a Codex-led container.** `$vs`, `$vss` and `$vsss` are Codex skills baked into the image at `/etc/codex/skills/{vs,vss,vsss}/SKILL.md` — the Admin scope root, so a project cannot add, shadow or disable one, and nothing is ever written into your Mac's `~/.codex` or `~/.agents` to make this work. Each SKILL.md tells Codex to read the single-sourced command body at `/usr/local/share/vibe/commands/<name>.md` and follow it verbatim, substituting a fixed table of Codex equivalents for the handful of Claude-only primitives those bodies use (`Agent`, `Skill`, `ScheduleWakeup`, and so on). The one primitive with no Codex equivalent at all is subagent dispatch — Codex's own `multi_agent` is pinned off in this image (see above) — so `/vs`'s Planner/Spec Critic/Generator/Tester/Evaluator roles become `vibe-delegate role <role> --model <astra|opus|sonnet|haiku|fable> --cwd <workspace>`: one fresh `codex exec` or `claude -p` process per role, run under the same hooks and firewall as the lead, never resuming or sharing a session with it. This ships the plumbing and its offline tests only; the first live Codex-led `/vs` trial is a later, separate item (see **MANUAL-TESTS Test 55**). Typing a bare `/vs` in Codex's own terminal is rejected by the composer before submission, but a line that starts with one leading space (` /vs …`) passes through as plain text with the space trimmed, and a managed `UserPromptSubmit` hook then adds context pointing the model at the `$vs` skill with the rest of the line as arguments — model-mediated, not guaranteed, so `$vs …` remains the reliable form. Nothing is written to the Mac's `~/.codex` to make this work, and the earlier `/prompts:`-based plan is withdrawn: Codex's custom-prompts directory is not read by the shipped binary. `/vs --panel N` has no subagents to fan out to either, so the image also ships `/usr/local/bin/codex-panel`: `codex-panel run --n N < diff` starts N independent, tool-less `codex exec` reviewers — one process each, a private temp cwd each, the same diff and a per-reviewer nonce on stdin — and prints one JSON object of verdicts for the chair to adjudicate. Its `--verify` mode drops `--ephemeral` so each reviewer's rollout is kept and can be proved to hold only its own nonce, which costs N live calls and leaves the diff and the nonces in `~/.codex/sessions` until you delete them, so it is an opt-in proof rather than the default. The same `$vs`/`$vss`/`$vsss` spelling also works when Claude Code is the lead: a synced CLAUDE.md fragment (`devcontainer/claude-md/dollar-prefix.md`) tells it that a prompt whose first token is one of those means invoke the matching command with the rest of the prompt as its arguments, verbatim.
 
 **The unattended supervisor.** A `$vsss` run is meant to outlive its own quota window, and Codex's CLI has no durable loop of its own, so the image ships one: `/usr/local/bin/codex-supervisor` (root-owned, like the guard chain). It speaks Codex's app-server protocol on stdio — newline-delimited JSON, not JSON-RPC 2.0 — starting ONE thread with `approvalPolicy: "never"` and `sandbox: "danger-full-access"` (the only mode that works here; the container has no user namespaces for Codex's own bubblewrap sandbox, and the real boundary is the container, firewall and hooks described above), then re-entering that same thread with the literal word `continue` until the run's exit report ends with `VSSS-EXIT: <reason>`. It runs the vendor binary as `codex app-server` with no `-c` flags and a four-variable environment, so the system requirements and managed hooks apply unaltered. The reason it uses the protocol rather than `codex exec` is that only the protocol carries `codexErrorInfo` and the rate-limit snapshot: a quota failure names itself and reports when the window resets, so the supervisor sleeps until then plus two minutes rather than guessing, backs off on transient failures, declines every approval request it is sent (nobody is there to answer), and writes its thread id and counters to `.vss/codex-supervisor.json` after every change — so a killed supervisor resumes the same thread instead of starting a new one. Ceilings on turns, resumes, quota waits, retries and wall time bound the whole thing, and `codex-supervisor status --state <file>` prints where a run got to. It is wired into a Codex-led launch by a later item; today it is shipped and tested offline against a stub app-server.
 
